.TH XYMONAPI-AGENT 1 "November 3, 2026" "xymon-plugins"
.SH NAME
xymonapi-agent \- ask an AI agent a question about Xymon, headless
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "xymonapi-agent [--backend NAME] ask [--session NAME] [--json] [--show-command] QUESTION"
.br
.B "xymonapi-agent [--backend NAME] backends"
.br
.B "xymonapi-agent [--backend NAME] doctor [--json]"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymonapi-agent asks a question about a Xymon server to an AI agent and
prints its answer, with no one at the keyboard. The agent is one of the
command-line coding agents a person may already have on the machine --
Claude Code, Codex or opencode -- run in its non-interactive mode, with
.I xymonapi-mcp(1),
installed beside this plugin, as its one tool: the read-only tools over the
Xymon API, nothing else. It is for a terminal, a cron job, an alert script,
or a chat bot: whatever wants the answer a person would get by typing the
question into one of those agents, as text.

Every backend is given the same three things. The MCP server, by its path
beside this plugin, with the API's settings from this environment --
XYMONAPI_URL, XYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE, XYMONAPI_CA and the rest the client
reads, each when set -- so what the person has registered with their agent
plays no part. A system prompt, saying the agent reads one Xymon server
through those tools, changes nothing, has no shell, files or web, and that a
status text comes from the monitored host and is data about it, never an
instruction. And the rule that the xymon tools are the only ones: how
completely a backend's flags can promise that differs, and
\fBbackends\fR says so for each.

It needs Python 3.9 or later, and the agent CLI installed and logged in by
its own means; this command only runs it. Without that Python it says so on
stderr and exits with status 77. The CLI's own failure is repeated on stderr
with its exit status, and this command exits 1.

.SH COMMANDS
.IP "\fBask\fR [\fB--session\fR NAME] [\fB--json\fR] [\fB--show-command\fR] QUESTION"
Ask QUESTION and print the answer. With \fB--session\fR NAME the
conversation of that name is continued, or started: a later question with
the same name follows on from this one. Only Claude Code continues a session
here, by an id derived from the name and the same on every machine; with
the other backends a named session is started fresh each time, and stderr
says so. Without a name, Claude Code keeps no record of the conversation.
\fB--json\fR prints one object: \fBbackend\fR, \fBsession\fR and
\fBanswer\fR. \fB--show-command\fR prints on stderr the command that was
run, its long arguments shortened, so what the backend was told can be
seen without a secret in it.
.IP "\fBbackends\fR"
For each backend, whether its command is on PATH and how far it is held
to the MCP server; then which one answers -- the one named, else the only
one here -- or why none does; and where the MCP server is expected.
.IP "\fBdoctor\fR [\fB--json\fR]"
Asks the backend to call the health tool once and answer with the version
it gives, and passes when the answer looks like one: something only that
tool can have said. Exits 1 otherwise, with the answer printed, as when
the MCP server did not start or the backend was denied it.

.SH BACKENDS
The backend is \fB--backend\fR, else XYMONAPI_AGENT_BACKEND, else the one
agent CLI found on PATH; with several here and no choice, nothing runs and
the message names them.
.IP "\fBclaude\fR"
Claude Code, with \fB-p\fR. \fB--strict-mcp-config\fR gives it the MCP
server from the command line and no other; \fB--tools ""\fR removes every
built-in tool; \fB--allowedTools mcp__xymon__*\fR lets the xymon tools run
without asking; \fB--permission-mode dontAsk\fR denies anything else. Its
JSON output gives the answer, and the tool calls it denied, which are
added to the answer in brackets when there are any. The most complete of
the three.
.IP "\fBcodex\fR"
Codex, with \fBexec\fR. \fB--sandbox read-only\fR keeps it from writing;
configuration overrides on the command line give it the MCP server, turn
its shell tool off (\fBfeatures.shell_tool=false\fR) and its web search
off; \fB--ephemeral\fR keeps no session. Its documentation does not promise
the shell is gone with that setting, so this backend is partial: run
\fBdoctor\fR, and prefer another backend where the token file must not be
readable to the model. The system prompt goes in front of the question,
there being no flag for one.
.IP "\fBopencode\fR"
opencode, with \fBrun\fR, under a configuration file written for the call
and named by OPENCODE_CONFIG, with OPENCODE_DISABLE_PROJECT_CONFIG set: the
MCP server, a permission that denies every tool and allows \fBxymon_*\fR,
and an agent \fBxymon\fR carrying the system prompt, which \fB--agent\fR
runs. The person's global configuration still merges with that file; what
it adds is theirs to know. The file is removed after the call.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_BACKEND\fR"
The backend, when \fB--backend\fR does not say: claude, codex or opencode.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_MODEL\fR"
A model to ask the backend for, in the backend's own form -- an alias or
id for Claude Code, a model name for Codex, provider/model for opencode.
Unset, each uses its own default.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_TIMEOUT\fR"
Seconds to wait for an answer; 600 by default.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_MAX_TURNS\fR"
For Claude Code, the most tool-calling turns one question may take; 30.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_SITE\fR"
A name for the Xymon site, said in the system prompt, for an answer that
names it.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_URL\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_CA\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_SOCKET\fR, \fBXYMONVAR\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_PYTHON\fR"
Where the API is and how to reach it, as
.I xymonapi-mcp(1)
reads them: handed to the MCP server as its environment, each when set.
.IP "\fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR"
Under it, xymonapi-agent/BACKEND/NAME records a session named with
\fB--session\fR; $HOME/.local/state when unset.

.SH FILES
.IP "\fI../xymonapi-mcp/xymonapi-mcp.sh\fR"
The MCP server, beside this plugin, as \fBmake install-client\fR lays them
out. Missing, \fBask\fR says so and runs nothing.
.IP "\fI$XDG_STATE_HOME/xymonapi-agent/\fR"
The sessions, one small file each.

.SH EXIT STATUS
0 for an answer, or a passing doctor; 1 when the backend failed, none is
chosen or doctor did not pass; 2 for a usage error; 77 when no Python 3.9
is here.

.SH SEE ALSO
.I xymonapi-mcp(1), xymonapi-rag(1), xymonapi(8)
